// Rep Max questions: Chemistry (AQA 7405). One question per line, no commas between lines.
// Fields: id (unique), s (subject), t (topic id from data/spec.js), lv (1 warm-up, 2 working set, 3 one-rep max),
// q (question; maths between $ signs, backslashes doubled), code (optional), o (four options), a (index 0-3 of the right one), x (explanation).
// A line that isn't valid is skipped and listed at the bottom of the home screen. Lines starting // are ignored.
{"id":"ch-001","s":"chem","t":"3.1.1","lv":1,"q":"What is the electron configuration of a Ca²⁺ ion?","o":["1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶","1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 4s²","1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 4s¹","1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d²"],"a":0,"x":"Calcium (Z = 20) is 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 4s². Forming Ca²⁺ removes both 4s electrons, leaving the same configuration as argon."}
{"id":"ch-002","s":"chem","t":"3.1.1","lv":1,"q":"In a time of flight mass spectrometer, ²⁰Ne⁺ and ²²Ne⁺ ions are accelerated to the same kinetic energy. Which statement is correct?","o":["²²Ne⁺ travels faster, so it reaches the detector first","Both reach the detector together because they have the same charge","²⁰Ne⁺ travels faster, so it reaches the detector first","²⁰Ne⁺ travels slower because lighter ions are deflected more"],"a":2,"x":"KE = ½mv², so for the same KE the lighter ion has the greater speed. It crosses the flight tube in less time and arrives first."}
{"id":"ch-003","s":"chem","t":"3.1.1","lv":2,"q":"Why is the first ionisation energy of aluminium lower than that of magnesium?","o":["Al has a smaller nuclear charge than Mg","Al's outer electron is removed from a 3p orbital containing a pair of electrons","Al's outer electron is in a 3p sub-shell, higher in energy and shielded by 3s","Al atoms are smaller than Mg atoms, so the outer electron is closer"],"a":2,"x":"Al's outer electron is in 3p, which is higher in energy than 3s and partly shielded by the 3s electrons. Al has more protons than Mg, not fewer."}
{"id":"ch-004","s":"chem","t":"3.1.1","lv":2,"q":"Boron has two isotopes: ¹⁰B (19.9%) and ¹¹B (80.1%). What is the relative atomic mass of boron to 1 decimal place?","o":["10.5","10.8","10.2","11.0"],"a":1,"x":"(10 × 19.9 + 11 × 80.1) ÷ 100 = 10.801, so 10.8. The simple mean (10.5) ignores abundances, and 10.2 swaps the abundances."}
{"id":"ch-005","s":"chem","t":"3.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A sample of magnesium gives mass spectrum peaks at m/z 24, 25 and 26 with relative heights 79, 10 and 11. What is the relative atomic mass to 1 decimal place?","o":["25.0","24.0","24.8","24.3"],"a":3,"x":"(24 × 79 + 25 × 10 + 26 × 11) ÷ (79 + 10 + 11) = 2432 ÷ 100 = 24.32, so 24.3. Weight each mass by its peak height, then divide by the total height."}
{"id":"ch-006","s":"chem","t":"3.1.2","lv":1,"q":"What amount, in mol, is present in 5.30 g of sodium carbonate, Na₂CO₃? (Na 23.0, C 12.0, O 16.0)","o":["0.0189","0.0500","0.500","0.106"],"a":1,"x":"Mr of Na₂CO₃ = 2(23.0) + 12.0 + 3(16.0) = 106.0. Moles = mass ÷ Mr = 5.30 ÷ 106.0 = 0.0500 mol."}
{"id":"ch-007","s":"chem","t":"3.1.2","lv":2,"q":"Ethanol is made by CH₃CH₂Cl + NaOH → CH₃CH₂OH + NaCl. What is the atom economy for ethanol? (H 1.0, C 12.0, O 16.0, Na 23.0, Cl 35.5)","o":["71.3%","56.0%","44.0%","88.0%"],"a":2,"x":"Atom economy = Mr of desired product ÷ total Mr of reactants × 100 = 46.0 ÷ (64.5 + 40.0) × 100 = 44.0%. Both reactants count in the total."}
{"id":"ch-008","s":"chem","t":"3.1.2","lv":2,"q":"A compound contains 40.0% C, 6.7% H and 53.3% O by mass and has Mr = 180.0. What is its molecular formula? (H 1.0, C 12.0, O 16.0)","o":["C₆H₁₂O₆","CH₂O","C₃H₆O₃","C₁₂H₂₄O₁₂"],"a":0,"x":"Moles ratio C : H : O = 3.33 : 6.7 : 3.33 = 1 : 2 : 1, so the empirical formula is CH₂O (Mr 30.0). 180.0 ÷ 30.0 = 6, giving C₆H₁₂O₆."}
{"id":"ch-009","s":"chem","t":"3.1.2","lv":2,"q":"25.0 cm³ of NaOH(aq) is neutralised by 20.0 cm³ of 0.100 mol dm⁻³ H₂SO₄. What is the concentration of the NaOH?","o":["0.160 mol dm⁻³","0.0800 mol dm⁻³","0.125 mol dm⁻³","0.320 mol dm⁻³"],"a":0,"x":"n(H₂SO₄) = 0.100 × 0.0200 = 2.00 × 10⁻³ mol. H₂SO₄ + 2NaOH, so n(NaOH) = 4.00 × 10⁻³ mol. Concentration = 4.00 × 10⁻³ ÷ 0.0250 = 0.160 mol dm⁻³."}
{"id":"ch-010","s":"chem","t":"3.1.2","lv":2,"q":"10.0 g of CaCO₃ is heated and 4.20 g of CaO is obtained (CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂). What is the percentage yield? (Ca 40.1, C 12.0, O 16.0)","o":["42.0%","133%","56.0%","74.9%"],"a":3,"x":"Mr CaCO₃ = 100.1, so 0.0999 mol gives a theoretical 0.0999 × 56.1 = 5.60 g CaO. Percentage yield = 4.20 ÷ 5.60 × 100 = 74.9%."}
{"id":"ch-011","s":"chem","t":"3.1.2","lv":3,"q":"0.250 g of a Group 2 metal reacts with excess HCl, giving 255 cm³ of H₂ at 298 K and 100 kPa. Using pV = nRT, which metal is it? (R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹; Ar: Be 9.0, Mg 24.3, Ca 40.1, Sr 87.6)","o":["Magnesium","Beryllium","Calcium","Strontium"],"a":0,"x":"V = 2.55 × 10⁻⁴ m³ and p = 1.00 × 10⁵ Pa, so n(H₂) = pV ÷ RT = 0.01030 mol. M + 2HCl → MCl₂ + H₂, so n(metal) = n(H₂) and Ar = 0.250 ÷ 0.01030 = 24.3: magnesium."}
{"id":"ch-012","s":"chem","t":"3.1.3","lv":1,"q":"Which species contains a dative covalent bond?","o":["NH₃","CH₄","MgCl₂","NH₄⁺"],"a":3,"x":"In NH₄⁺ the N atom donates its lone pair to an H⁺ ion, so both electrons in that N–H bond come from nitrogen. NH₃ and CH₄ have only ordinary covalent bonds."}
{"id":"ch-013","s":"chem","t":"3.1.3","lv":1,"q":"What is the H–N–H bond angle in a molecule of ammonia, NH₃?","o":["107°","109.5°","104.5°","120°"],"a":0,"x":"NH₃ has three bonding pairs and one lone pair. The lone pair repels more strongly than a bonding pair, squeezing the angle from 109.5° to 107°."}
{"id":"ch-014","s":"chem","t":"3.1.3","lv":1,"q":"Which molecule contains polar bonds but is non-polar overall?","o":["CHCl₃","CCl₄","NH₃","H₂O"],"a":1,"x":"CCl₄ is tetrahedral and symmetrical, so the four C–Cl dipoles cancel. In CHCl₃ the C–H and C–Cl dipoles differ, so they do not cancel; pyramidal NH₃ and bent H₂O are also polar."}
{"id":"ch-015","s":"chem","t":"3.1.3","lv":1,"q":"Why does water have a higher boiling point than hydrogen sulfide, H₂S?","o":["Hydrogen bonds form between water molecules","O–H covalent bonds are stronger than S–H bonds and must be broken","Water molecules have more electrons, so London forces are stronger","Water molecules are larger than H₂S molecules"],"a":0,"x":"Oxygen is small, very electronegative and has lone pairs, so water forms hydrogen bonds. Boiling breaks intermolecular forces, not covalent bonds. H₂S has more electrons."}
{"id":"ch-016","s":"chem","t":"3.1.3","lv":2,"q":"Why does magnesium have a higher melting point than sodium?","o":["Mg atoms have a greater mass, so there are stronger London forces","Mg has two outer electrons, so it forms stronger covalent bonds","Mg²⁺ is smaller and more highly charged, and gives two delocalised electrons","Mg has more neutrons, so its ions are held together more tightly"],"a":2,"x":"Metallic bonding is the attraction between cations and delocalised electrons. Mg²⁺ has a higher charge, a smaller radius and gives two electrons per atom, so the attraction is stronger."}
{"id":"ch-017","s":"chem","t":"3.1.3","lv":3,"q":"XeF₄ has four bonding pairs and two lone pairs around the Xe atom. What is its shape and F–Xe–F bond angle?","o":["Tetrahedral, 109.5°","Square planar, 90°","Seesaw, 90° and 120°","Octahedral, 90°"],"a":1,"x":"Six electron pairs arrange octahedrally. The two lone pairs sit opposite each other, to minimise repulsion, leaving the four F atoms in a flat square with 90° angles."}
{"id":"ch-018","s":"chem","t":"3.1.4","lv":1,"q":"Which equation represents the standard enthalpy of formation of methane?","o":["C(graphite) + 2H₂(g) → CH₄(g)","C(g) + 4H(g) → CH₄(g)","C(graphite) + 4H(g) → CH₄(g)","2C(graphite) + 4H₂(g) → 2CH₄(g)"],"a":0,"x":"Formation is 1 mol of the compound made from its elements in their standard states: carbon as graphite and hydrogen as H₂(g). Gaseous atoms are not standard states."}
{"id":"ch-019","s":"chem","t":"3.1.4","lv":2,"q":"Burning 1.15 g of ethanol (Mr = 46.0) raises the temperature of 200 g of water by 14.0 °C. c = 4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹. What is the enthalpy of combustion?","o":["+468 kJ mol⁻¹","−11.7 kJ mol⁻¹","−468 kJ mol⁻¹","−2.69 kJ mol⁻¹"],"a":2,"x":"q = mcΔT = 200 × 4.18 × 14.0 = 11 704 J = 11.7 kJ. n(ethanol) = 1.15 ÷ 46.0 = 0.0250 mol. ΔH = −11.7 ÷ 0.0250 = −468 kJ mol⁻¹, negative because the water warms up."}
{"id":"ch-020","s":"chem","t":"3.1.4","lv":2,"q":"Given ΔfH(CaCO₃) = −1207, ΔfH(CaO) = −635 and ΔfH(CO₂) = −394 kJ mol⁻¹, what is ΔH for CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂?","o":["+178 kJ mol⁻¹","−178 kJ mol⁻¹","+966 kJ mol⁻¹","−2236 kJ mol⁻¹"],"a":0,"x":"ΔH = ΣΔfH(products) − ΣΔfH(reactants) = (−635 − 394) − (−1207) = +178 kJ mol⁻¹. Reversing the subtraction gives the sign error −178."}
{"id":"ch-021","s":"chem","t":"3.1.4","lv":2,"q":"Mean bond enthalpies (kJ mol⁻¹): C–H 413, Cl–Cl 243, C–Cl 346, H–Cl 432. What is ΔH for CH₄ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + HCl?","o":["+122 kJ mol⁻¹","−365 kJ mol⁻¹","−122 kJ mol⁻¹","−778 kJ mol⁻¹"],"a":2,"x":"Bonds broken: C–H + Cl–Cl = 413 + 243 = 656. Bonds made: C–Cl + H–Cl = 346 + 432 = 778. ΔH = broken − made = 656 − 778 = −122 kJ mol⁻¹."}
{"id":"ch-022","s":"chem","t":"3.1.4","lv":3,"q":"ΔcH (kJ mol⁻¹): C(s) = −394, H₂(g) = −286, C₃H₈(g) = −2219. What is the standard enthalpy of formation of propane, C₃H₈(g)?","o":["+107 kJ mol⁻¹","+1539 kJ mol⁻¹","−4545 kJ mol⁻¹","−107 kJ mol⁻¹"],"a":3,"x":"3C + 4H₂ → C₃H₈. ΔfH = ΣΔcH(reactants) − ΣΔcH(products) = [3(−394) + 4(−286)] − (−2219) = −2326 + 2219 = −107 kJ mol⁻¹."}
{"id":"ch-023","s":"chem","t":"3.1.5","lv":1,"q":"How does a catalyst increase the rate of a reaction?","o":["It provides an alternative route with a lower activation energy","It increases the energy of every collision","It increases the enthalpy change so more product forms","It increases the number of reactant molecules present"],"a":0,"x":"A catalyst offers an alternative pathway with a lower activation energy, so a greater proportion of collisions are successful at the same temperature."}
{"id":"ch-024","s":"chem","t":"3.1.5","lv":2,"q":"The temperature of a gas mixture is raised. Which statement about the Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution is correct?","o":["The peak moves to lower energy and is higher; the area stays the same","The peak moves to higher energy and is lower; the area increases","The activation energy moves to a lower energy on the axis","The peak moves to higher energy and is lower; the area stays the same"],"a":3,"x":"Heating shifts the peak right and flattens it. The area equals the total number of molecules, which does not change, and Ea stays put, so more molecules have E ≥ Ea."}
{"id":"ch-025","s":"chem","t":"3.1.5","lv":3,"q":"A 10 K rise from 298 K roughly doubles the rate of a reaction. What is the main reason?","o":["The collision frequency doubles","The activation energy is halved","The concentration of the reactants increases","A much larger proportion of collisions have energy ≥ the activation energy"],"a":3,"x":"Collision frequency rises by less than 2% for a 10 K rise. The exponential increase in the fraction of molecules with E ≥ Ea is what roughly doubles the rate."}
{"id":"ch-026","s":"chem","t":"3.1.6","lv":1,"q":"N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g), ΔH = −92 kJ mol⁻¹. Which change moves the equilibrium position to the right?","o":["Increasing the temperature","Decreasing the pressure","Adding an iron catalyst","Increasing the pressure"],"a":3,"x":"There are 4 mol of gas on the left and 2 mol on the right. Higher pressure favours the side with fewer gas moles. Heating favours the endothermic (left) direction. A catalyst does not shift the position."}
{"id":"ch-027","s":"chem","t":"3.1.6","lv":1,"q":"What are the units of Kc for 2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g)?","o":["mol dm⁻³","mol⁻² dm⁶","mol⁻¹ dm³","no units"],"a":2,"x":"Kc = [SO₃]² ÷ ([SO₂]²[O₂]). Units: (mol dm⁻³)² ÷ (mol dm⁻³)³ = (mol dm⁻³)⁻¹ = mol⁻¹ dm³."}
{"id":"ch-028","s":"chem","t":"3.1.6","lv":1,"q":"The forward reaction of an equilibrium is exothermic. What happens when the temperature is increased?","o":["Kc increases and the position shifts to the right","Kc is unchanged and the position shifts to the left","Kc decreases and the position shifts to the left","Kc is unchanged and the position shifts to the right"],"a":2,"x":"The system opposes the rise by favouring the endothermic (reverse) reaction. The equilibrium position moves left and Kc, which depends only on temperature, decreases."}
{"id":"ch-029","s":"chem","t":"3.1.6","lv":3,"q":"2.00 mol of A and 2.00 mol of B are mixed in 2.00 dm³ at constant T: A + B ⇌ C. At equilibrium there is 1.20 mol of C. What is Kc?","o":["1.88 dm³ mol⁻¹","3.75 dm³ mol⁻¹","0.267 mol dm⁻³","0.938 dm³ mol⁻¹"],"a":1,"x":"At equilibrium A = B = 0.80 mol, C = 1.20 mol. Concentrations: 0.40, 0.40, 0.60 mol dm⁻³. Kc = 0.60 ÷ (0.40 × 0.40) = 3.75 dm³ mol⁻¹. Using moles instead of concentrations gives 1.88."}
{"id":"ch-030","s":"chem","t":"3.1.7","lv":1,"q":"What is the oxidation state of oxygen in hydrogen peroxide, H₂O₂?","o":["−2","−1","0","+1"],"a":1,"x":"Hydrogen is +1, and the molecule is neutral, so the two oxygens total −2, giving −1 each. Peroxides are an exception to the usual −2 for oxygen."}
{"id":"ch-031","s":"chem","t":"3.1.7","lv":2,"q":"In Fe₂O₃ + 3CO → 2Fe + 3CO₂, which statement is correct?","o":["Fe₂O₃ is the reducing agent because iron goes from +3 to 0","Carbon is reduced because it goes from +2 to +4","Oxygen is oxidised because it is lost from Fe₂O₃","CO is the reducing agent because carbon goes from +2 to +4"],"a":3,"x":"Carbon's oxidation state rises from +2 to +4, so CO is oxidised and is the reducing agent. Iron falls from +3 to 0, so it is reduced and Fe₂O₃ is the oxidising agent."}
{"id":"ch-032","s":"chem","t":"3.1.7","lv":2,"q":"Which is the correct half-equation for the reduction of dichromate(VI) ions in acid?","o":["Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O + 6e⁻","Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 6e⁻ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O","Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 3e⁻ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O","Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 7H⁺ + 6e⁻ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O"],"a":1,"x":"Each Cr goes from +6 to +3, so 6 electrons are gained, on the left. Check: charge on left is −2 + 14 − 6 = +6, and on right 2 × 3+ = +6. 14 H⁺ balance the 7 H₂O."}
{"id":"ch-033","s":"chem","t":"3.1.7","lv":3,"q":"MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O and Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺ + e⁻. How many moles of Fe²⁺ are oxidised by 20.0 cm³ of 0.0200 mol dm⁻³ MnO₄⁻?","o":["4.00 × 10⁻⁴ mol","8.00 × 10⁻⁵ mol","2.00 × 10⁻³ mol","2.00 × 10⁻² mol"],"a":2,"x":"n(MnO₄⁻) = 0.0200 × 0.0200 = 4.00 × 10⁻⁴ mol. Each MnO₄⁻ accepts 5 electrons, and each Fe²⁺ gives 1, so n(Fe²⁺) = 5 × 4.00 × 10⁻⁴ = 2.00 × 10⁻³ mol."}
{"id":"ch-034","s":"chem","t":"3.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Why does atomic radius decrease across Period 3 from sodium to chlorine?","o":["Shielding increases while nuclear charge stays the same","The number of electron shells decreases","Nuclear charge increases while shielding stays about the same","Extra electrons are added to the inner shells"],"a":2,"x":"Across a period, electrons go into the same shell, so shielding is almost constant. The increasing nuclear charge pulls the outer electrons in more strongly."}
{"id":"ch-035","s":"chem","t":"3.2.1","lv":2,"q":"Why is the first ionisation energy of sulfur lower than that of phosphorus?","o":["S has a smaller nuclear charge than P","S has its outer electron in a 3d sub-shell","In S, two electrons share a 3p orbital and repel each other","S has more electron shells than P"],"a":2,"x":"P has 3p³, with each electron in its own orbital. S has 3p⁴, where one orbital holds a pair. Repulsion between the paired electrons makes one easier to remove."}
{"id":"ch-036","s":"chem","t":"3.2.1","lv":2,"q":"Why does sulfur (S₈) have a higher melting point than white phosphorus (P₄)?","o":["S–S covalent bonds are stronger than P–P bonds","Sulfur has a giant covalent structure","S₈ molecules are polar, so have permanent dipole forces","S₈ molecules are larger, so there are stronger London forces"],"a":3,"x":"Both are simple molecular, so melting breaks only intermolecular forces. S₈ has more electrons than P₄, so the London forces between molecules are stronger."}
{"id":"ch-037","s":"chem","t":"3.2.2","lv":1,"q":"Why does reactivity increase down Group 2?","o":["Atoms are larger with more shielding, so outer electrons are lost more easily","Nuclear charge decreases, so outer electrons are lost more easily","Atomic radius decreases, so outer electrons are lost more easily","Electronegativity increases, so atoms attract electrons more strongly"],"a":0,"x":"Down the group the outer electrons are further from the nucleus and more shielded. Less energy is needed to remove them, so the ionisation energies fall and reactivity rises."}
{"id":"ch-038","s":"chem","t":"3.2.2","lv":2,"q":"Magnesium is heated in steam and burns with a bright white light. Which equation represents this reaction?","o":["Mg(s) + 2H₂O(g) → Mg(OH)₂(s) + H₂(g)","Mg(s) + H₂O(g) → MgO(s) + H₂(g)","Mg(s) + H₂O(g) → MgO(s) + O₂(g)","2Mg(s) + H₂O(g) → Mg₂O(s) + H₂(g)"],"a":1,"x":"In steam, magnesium burns to give white magnesium oxide and hydrogen. Mg(OH)₂ is the product with cold water, which reacts very slowly. The oxide contains Mg²⁺ and O²⁻, so it is MgO, and no O₂ is made."}
{"id":"ch-039","s":"chem","t":"3.2.2","lv":2,"q":"1.85 g of Ca(OH)₂ (Mr = 74.1) is neutralised by 0.500 mol dm⁻³ HCl. What volume of acid is needed?","o":["99.9 cm³","49.9 cm³","130 cm³","200 cm³"],"a":0,"x":"n(Ca(OH)₂) = 1.85 ÷ 74.1 = 0.02497 mol. Ca(OH)₂ + 2HCl, so n(HCl) = 0.04994 mol. V = 0.04994 ÷ 0.500 = 0.0999 dm³ = 99.9 cm³."}
{"id":"ch-040","s":"chem","t":"3.2.3","lv":1,"q":"Chlorine water is added to aqueous potassium bromide. What is observed, and what is the ionic equation?","o":["The solution turns orange; Br₂ + 2Cl⁻ → 2Br⁻ + Cl₂","A cream precipitate forms; Cl₂ + 2Br⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + Br₂","There is no reaction, as chlorine is less reactive than bromine","The solution turns orange; Cl₂ + 2Br⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + Br₂"],"a":3,"x":"Chlorine is a stronger oxidising agent than bromine, so it oxidises Br⁻ ions to Br₂, which turns the solution orange. No precipitate forms: a cream precipitate is the silver nitrate test for Br⁻."}
{"id":"ch-041","s":"chem","t":"3.2.3","lv":1,"q":"A halide solution is acidified with dilute nitric acid and silver nitrate is added. A cream precipitate forms, which dissolves in concentrated NH₃ but not in dilute NH₃. Which halide ion is present?","o":["Cl⁻","Br⁻","I⁻","F⁻"],"a":1,"x":"AgBr is cream and dissolves only in concentrated ammonia. AgCl is white and dissolves in dilute ammonia, AgI is yellow and insoluble, and AgF is soluble so gives no precipitate."}
{"id":"ch-042","s":"chem","t":"3.2.3","lv":2,"q":"Solid sodium iodide reacts with concentrated sulfuric acid. Which product has a smell of bad eggs?","o":["SO₂","HI","I₂","H₂S"],"a":3,"x":"Iodide ions are strong enough reducing agents to reduce sulfur from +6 to −2, forming H₂S. SO₂ is choking, I₂ is a purple vapour or black solid, and HI is a colourless gas."}
{"id":"ch-043","s":"chem","t":"3.2.3","lv":2,"q":"Chlorine reacts with cold, dilute aqueous NaOH: Cl₂ + 2NaOH → NaCl + NaClO + H₂O. What happens to the chlorine?","o":["It is reduced only, from 0 to −1","It is oxidised only, from 0 to +1","It disproportionates, from 0 to −1 and +5","It disproportionates, from 0 to −1 and +1"],"a":3,"x":"In NaCl chlorine is −1 (reduced) and in NaClO it is +1 (oxidised), so the same element is both oxidised and reduced. The +5 state forms with hot NaOH."}
{"id":"ch-044","s":"chem","t":"3.3.1","lv":1,"q":"What is the IUPAC name of CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₃?","o":["3-methylbutane","2-methylpropane","2-methylbutane","2-ethylpropane"],"a":2,"x":"The longest chain is four carbons (butane). A CH₃ branch is on carbon 2 when numbering from the end that gives the lower number, so it is 2-methylbutane."}
{"id":"ch-045","s":"chem","t":"3.3.1","lv":2,"q":"Which compound shows E/Z isomerism?","o":["But-1-ene","2-methylbut-2-ene","Propene","Pent-2-ene"],"a":3,"x":"E/Z needs two different groups on each carbon of C=C. Pent-2-ene has H and CH₃ on one carbon and H and C₂H₅ on the other. The others each have a carbon with two identical groups."}
{"id":"ch-046","s":"chem","t":"3.3.1","lv":1,"q":"What is formed when the Cl–Cl bond undergoes homolytic fission?","o":["A Cl⁺ ion and a Cl⁻ ion","Two chlorine radicals, each with one unpaired electron","Two Cl⁻ ions","Two Cl⁺ ions"],"a":1,"x":"In homolytic fission the bonding pair is shared equally, one electron going to each atom. This gives two radicals. Heterolytic fission gives a pair of ions."}
{"id":"ch-047","s":"chem","t":"3.3.1","lv":3,"q":"How many different alkenes, including E/Z isomers, have the molecular formula C₄H₈?","o":["2","3","4","5"],"a":2,"x":"But-1-ene, methylpropene, (E)-but-2-ene and (Z)-but-2-ene. There are 3 structural isomers, but but-2-ene exists as two stereoisomers, so the total is 4."}
{"id":"ch-048","s":"chem","t":"3.3.2","lv":1,"q":"Which describes the molecules that leave near the top of a fractionating column?","o":["Short chains, with strong London forces and low boiling points","Short chains, with weak London forces and low boiling points","Long chains, with strong London forces and high boiling points","Long chains, with weak London forces and low boiling points"],"a":1,"x":"Short molecules have fewer electrons and weaker London forces, so they have low boiling points and stay as vapour up to the cooler top of the column."}
{"id":"ch-049","s":"chem","t":"3.3.2","lv":1,"q":"Which equation correctly represents the thermal cracking of decane?","o":["C₁₀H₂₂ → C₆H₁₄ + 2C₂H₄","C₁₀H₂₂ → C₆H₁₂ + 2C₂H₄","C₁₀H₂₂ → C₆H₁₄ + C₂H₄","C₁₀H₂₂ → C₇H₁₆ + 2C₂H₄"],"a":0,"x":"Count atoms. Right side: C = 6 + 4 = 10, H = 14 + 8 = 22. The other options give 20 H, 8 C and 11 C respectively."}
{"id":"ch-050","s":"chem","t":"3.3.2","lv":2,"q":"Which equation shows a propagation step in the chlorination of methane?","o":["CH₃· + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + Cl·","Cl₂ → 2Cl·","CH₃· + Cl· → CH₃Cl","2CH₃· → C₂H₆"],"a":0,"x":"In propagation a radical reacts and a new radical is made. Cl₂ → 2Cl· is initiation. The other two options use up two radicals and make none, so they are termination steps."}
{"id":"ch-051","s":"chem","t":"3.3.3","lv":1,"q":"Which reagent and conditions convert 1-bromopropane into propan-1-ol?","o":["Ethanolic KOH, heat under reflux","Aqueous NaOH, heat under reflux","Concentrated H₂SO₄, heat","Acidified K₂Cr₂O₇, heat under reflux"],"a":1,"x":"Aqueous hydroxide ions act as nucleophiles and substitute the bromine. Ethanolic KOH causes elimination to propene instead."}
{"id":"ch-052","s":"chem","t":"3.3.3","lv":2,"q":"2-bromopropane is heated with ethanolic KOH. Which describes the reaction?","o":["Nucleophilic substitution, with OH⁻ acting as a nucleophile to form propan-2-ol","Electrophilic addition, with OH⁻ acting as an electrophile to form propene","Elimination, with OH⁻ acting as a nucleophile to form propene","Elimination, with OH⁻ acting as a base to form propene"],"a":3,"x":"Ethanolic conditions favour elimination. OH⁻ removes an H⁺ from a carbon next to C–Br, so it acts as a base, and HBr is lost to give propene."}
{"id":"ch-053","s":"chem","t":"3.3.3","lv":2,"q":"Which equation shows chlorine radicals being regenerated during the breakdown of ozone?","o":["Cl· + O₃ → ClO· + O₂","CF₂Cl₂ → CF₂Cl· + Cl·","ClO· + O₃ → Cl· + 2O₂","Cl· + Cl· → Cl₂"],"a":2,"x":"Cl· is used up in Cl· + O₃ → ClO· + O₂, then regenerated in ClO· + O₃ → Cl· + 2O₂. This makes it a catalyst, so one radical can destroy many ozone molecules."}
{"id":"ch-054","s":"chem","t":"3.3.4","lv":1,"q":"What is observed when an alkene is shaken with bromine water?","o":["Orange to green","Colourless to orange","Orange to colourless","Orange to purple"],"a":2,"x":"Bromine adds across the C=C double bond, forming a colourless dibromoalkane, so the orange bromine water is decolourised."}
{"id":"ch-055","s":"chem","t":"3.3.4","lv":2,"q":"What is the major product when propene reacts with HBr, and why?","o":["2-bromopropane, via a more stable secondary carbocation","1-bromopropane, via a more stable primary carbocation","2-bromopropane, via a more stable primary carbocation","1-bromopropane, via a more stable secondary carbocation"],"a":0,"x":"H⁺ adds to the end carbon, giving the secondary carbocation CH₃CH⁺CH₃. Alkyl groups push electron density towards the positive carbon, so a secondary carbocation is more stable than a primary one."}
{"id":"ch-056","s":"chem","t":"3.3.4","lv":1,"q":"A section of an addition polymer is –CH₂–CHCl–CH₂–CHCl–CH₂–CHCl–. What is the monomer?","o":["CH₂=CHCl","ClCH=CHCl","CH₂=CCl₂","CH₃CH₂Cl"],"a":0,"x":"The chain repeats –CH₂–CHCl–. Opening the C=C bond of CH₂=CHCl (chloroethene) and joining many units gives this backbone of single bonds."}
{"id":"ch-057","s":"chem","t":"3.3.5","lv":1,"q":"Which conditions are used to ferment glucose to ethanol?","o":["Yeast, about 35 °C, excess air","Concentrated H₃PO₄, 300 °C, 60 atm","Nickel catalyst, 150 °C, hydrogen","Yeast, about 35 °C, no oxygen"],"a":3,"x":"Yeast enzymes convert glucose to ethanol and CO₂ at about 30–40 °C. Oxygen must be excluded, otherwise the ethanol is oxidised to ethanoic acid."}
{"id":"ch-058","s":"chem","t":"3.3.5","lv":2,"q":"Ethanol is heated under reflux with excess acidified potassium dichromate(VI). What is the product and colour change?","o":["Ethanal; orange to green","Ethanoic acid; orange to green","Ethanoic acid; green to orange","Ethanal; green to orange"],"a":1,"x":"Reflux with excess oxidising agent oxidises a primary alcohol fully to the carboxylic acid. Cr₂O₇²⁻ (orange) is reduced to Cr³⁺ (green). Ethanal requires distilling off as it forms."}
{"id":"ch-059","s":"chem","t":"3.3.5","lv":3,"q":"Butan-2-ol is dehydrated with concentrated H₃PO₄. How many different alkene products, including stereoisomers, can form?","o":["1","3","2","4"],"a":1,"x":"Removing OH from C2 with H from C1 gives but-1-ene. Removing H from C3 gives but-2-ene, which has two stereoisomers, E and Z. That makes 3 products in total."}
{"id":"ch-060","s":"chem","t":"3.3.6","lv":1,"q":"In the mass spectrum of an organic compound, what does the m/z value of the molecular ion peak tell you?","o":["The number of carbon atoms in the compound","The relative molecular mass of the compound","The relative atomic mass of the heaviest atom","The mass of the most stable fragment"],"a":1,"x":"The molecular ion M⁺ is the whole molecule with one electron removed, so its m/z value equals the relative molecular mass of the compound."}
{"id":"ch-061","s":"chem","t":"3.3.6","lv":3,"q":"A compound has a molecular ion peak at m/z 74. Its IR spectrum has a very broad absorption at 2500–3000 cm⁻¹ and a strong sharp absorption near 1710 cm⁻¹. What is it? (H 1.0, C 12.0, O 16.0)","o":["Propanoic acid, CH₃CH₂COOH","Methyl ethanoate, CH₃COOCH₃","Butan-1-ol, CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂OH","Propanal, CH₃CH₂CHO"],"a":0,"x":"A broad band at 2500–3000 cm⁻¹ is the O–H of a carboxylic acid, and 1710 cm⁻¹ is C=O. Propanoic acid has Mr = 74. The ester lacks the O–H band, butan-1-ol lacks C=O, and propanal has Mr = 58."}
{"id":"ch-062","s":"chem","t":"3.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Which statement correctly describes two isotopes of the same element?","o":["Same number of protons, different number of neutrons","Same number of neutrons, different number of protons","Same number of protons and neutrons, different number of electrons","Same mass number, different number of protons"],"a":0,"x":"Isotopes are atoms of the same element, so they have the same atomic number (protons) but different mass numbers, which means different numbers of neutrons. Same mass number with different protons describes different elements."}
{"id":"ch-063","s":"chem","t":"3.1.2","lv":1,"q":"Which is the correct definition of relative atomic mass, Ar?","o":["Weighted mean mass of an atom compared with the mass of a carbon-12 atom","Mass of one atom compared with the mass of one hydrogen-1 atom","Mean mass of one mole of atoms of the element in grams","Weighted mean mass of an atom compared with 1/12 of the mass of a carbon-12 atom"],"a":3,"x":"Ar is a weighted mean over the isotopes, compared with 1/12 of the mass of a carbon-12 atom. Leaving out the 1/12 or using hydrogen-1 is wrong, and a mass in grams is molar mass, which has units; Ar has none."}
{"id":"ch-064","s":"chem","t":"3.1.1","lv":2,"q":"The first five ionisation energies of an element in kJ mol⁻¹ are 578, 1817, 2745, 11577 and 14842. In which group of the periodic table is the element?","o":["Group 2","Group 3","Group 4","Group 5"],"a":1,"x":"The big jump comes between the 3rd and 4th values. The 4th electron is taken from an inner shell, closer to the nucleus, so the element has 3 outer electrons and is in Group 3 (this is aluminium)."}
{"id":"ch-066","s":"chem","t":"3.1.2","lv":1,"q":"What is the empirical formula of a compound?","o":["The simplest whole number ratio of atoms of each element in the compound","The actual number of atoms of each element in one molecule of the compound","The simplest whole number ratio of the masses of each element in the compound","The formula showing which atoms are bonded to which in the molecule"],"a":0,"x":"Empirical formula is the simplest whole number ratio of atoms of each element. The actual numbers are the molecular formula, and mass ratios must be divided by Ar to get a mole ratio first."}
{"id":"ch-067","s":"chem","t":"3.1.2","lv":1,"q":"A student uses pV = nRT with R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹. Which pair of units should be used for p and V?","o":["p in kPa and V in m³","p in Pa and V in m³","p in Pa and V in dm³","p in kPa and V in cm³"],"a":1,"x":"R is in J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ and 1 J = 1 Pa m³, so use p in Pa and V in m³ (with T in K). Each of the other pairs makes the calculated n wrong by a factor of 1000."}
{"id":"ch-068","s":"chem","t":"3.1.2","lv":2,"q":"Which statement about a chemical reaction is correct?","o":["A reaction with 100% atom economy must have a percentage yield of 100%","Atom economy decreases when product is lost during purification","A reaction can have a percentage yield of 100% and still have a low atom economy","A low percentage yield means the reaction has a low atom economy"],"a":2,"x":"Atom economy depends only on the balanced equation (how much of the reactants ends up in the desired product). Percentage yield depends on what is actually collected. They are independent."}
{"id":"ch-069","s":"chem","t":"3.1.3","lv":1,"q":"What holds the particles together in solid sodium chloride?","o":["Shared pairs of electrons between sodium and chlorine atoms","Attraction between positive ions and delocalised electrons","London forces between NaCl molecules","Electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions"],"a":3,"x":"Sodium chloride is a giant ionic lattice of Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions held by strong electrostatic attraction. It contains no molecules, no shared pairs and no delocalised electrons."}
{"id":"ch-070","s":"chem","t":"3.1.3","lv":1,"q":"Which species has a bond angle of 109.5°?","o":["NH₄⁺","NH₃","H₂O","BF₃"],"a":0,"x":"NH₄⁺ has four bonding pairs and no lone pairs, so it is tetrahedral at 109.5°. NH₃ (107°) and H₂O (104.5°) are squeezed by lone pairs, and BF₃ is trigonal planar (120°)."}
{"id":"ch-071","s":"chem","t":"3.1.3","lv":2,"q":"Why does graphite conduct electricity but diamond does not?","o":["Weak forces between its layers let ions move through graphite","Each carbon uses three electrons in bonding, leaving one per atom delocalised","Graphite has positive ions in a sea of electrons, like a metal","Diamond has stronger covalent bonds, so its electrons cannot move"],"a":1,"x":"In graphite each carbon bonds to three others, leaving one electron free to move along the layers. In diamond all four outer electrons are in localised bonds. Weak forces between layers explain softness, not conduction."}
{"id":"ch-072","s":"chem","t":"3.1.3","lv":3,"q":"NH₃ and BF₃ join by a dative bond to form H₃N→BF₃. How does the shape around N and around B change?","o":["N: stays pyramidal; B: trigonal planar to tetrahedral","N: pyramidal to tetrahedral; B: stays trigonal planar","N: pyramidal to tetrahedral; B: trigonal planar to tetrahedral","N: pyramidal to tetrahedral; B: trigonal planar to pyramidal"],"a":2,"x":"N's lone pair becomes the bonding pair of the dative bond, so N goes from 3 bonding pairs and 1 lone pair (pyramidal) to 4 bonding pairs (tetrahedral). B gains a fourth bonding pair and has no lone pair, so it also becomes tetrahedral."}
{"id":"ch-073","s":"chem","t":"3.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Which Period 3 element has the highest melting point?","o":["Magnesium","Silicon","Aluminium","Sulfur"],"a":1,"x":"Silicon has a giant covalent lattice, so a lot of energy is needed to break its many strong covalent bonds. Magnesium and aluminium are metals with lower melting points, and sulfur is simple molecular."}
{"id":"ch-074","s":"chem","t":"3.2.1","lv":2,"q":"Why is the melting point of argon much lower than that of silicon?","o":["Argon has weaker covalent bonds between its atoms than silicon","Argon atoms have a lower mass than silicon atoms","Argon has weaker metallic bonding as it has fewer delocalised electrons","Argon has weak London forces; silicon has a giant lattice of covalent bonds"],"a":3,"x":"Argon is monatomic, with only weak London forces between atoms, so little energy is needed to melt it. It has no covalent bonds between atoms, and its atoms are heavier than silicon's, not lighter."}
{"id":"ch-075","s":"chem","t":"3.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Which equation represents the first ionisation energy of magnesium?","o":["Mg(s) → Mg⁺(g) + e⁻","Mg(g) → Mg²⁺(g) + 2e⁻","Mg(g) → Mg⁺(g) + e⁻","Mg⁺(g) → Mg²⁺(g) + e⁻"],"a":2,"x":"First ionisation energy is for one mole of gaseous atoms each losing one electron: Mg(g) → Mg⁺(g) + e⁻. Mg(s) has the wrong state, Mg⁺ → Mg²⁺ is the second ionisation, and Mg → Mg²⁺ removes two electrons."}
{"id":"ch-076","s":"chem","t":"3.2.1","lv":2,"q":"Why does argon have the highest first ionisation energy in Period 3?","o":["Greatest nuclear charge in Period 3, with similar shielding","Its outer electrons are in a higher energy level than chlorine's","Its paired 3p electrons repel each other less than unpaired ones","It has more electron shells than the other Period 3 atoms"],"a":0,"x":"Across Period 3 the outer electrons stay in the third shell, so shielding is similar while nuclear charge rises. Argon has the most protons (18), so its outer electrons are held most strongly. Paired electrons repel more, not less."}
{"id":"ch-078","s":"chem","t":"3.1.1","lv":3,"q":"The second ionisation energy of sodium (4562 kJ mol⁻¹) is much greater than that of magnesium (1451 kJ mol⁻¹). What is the main reason?","o":["Na⁺ has more protons than Mg⁺, so its electrons are attracted more strongly","Sodium has stronger metallic bonding than magnesium, so more energy is needed","Na⁺ loses a 2p electron, closer to the nucleus and less shielded than 3s","Na⁺ is a larger ion than Mg⁺, so its outer electron is held more tightly"],"a":2,"x":"Na⁺ is 1s² 2s² 2p⁶, so its next electron comes from the second shell, close to the nucleus with little shielding. Mg⁺ is 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s¹, so it loses a 3s electron. Mg has the greater nuclear charge (12 against 11)."}
{"id":"ch-079","s":"chem","t":"3.3.1","lv":1,"q":"Which statement defines structural isomers?","o":["Same structural formula but a different arrangement of atoms in space","Same empirical formula but different molecular formulae","Members of a homologous series that differ by a CH₂ unit","Same molecular formula but different structural formulae"],"a":3,"x":"Structural isomers have the same molecular formula but different structural formulae: the atoms are joined in a different order. Same structural formula with a different arrangement in space describes stereoisomers; differing by CH₂ describes a homologous series."}
{"id":"ch-080","s":"chem","t":"3.3.1","lv":1,"q":"Hydrogen bromide undergoes heterolytic fission. What is formed?","o":["H⁺ and Br⁻","H• and Br•","H⁻ and Br⁺","H₂ and Br₂"],"a":0,"x":"In heterolytic fission both electrons of the bond go to one atom. Bromine is more electronegative, so it takes the pair and forms Br⁻, leaving H⁺. Radicals H• and Br• form in homolytic fission."}
{"id":"ch-081","s":"chem","t":"3.3.1","lv":2,"q":"What is the IUPAC name of CH₃CH₂C(CH₃)=CH₂?","o":["3-methylbut-1-ene","2-methylbut-1-ene","2-methylbut-2-ene","2-ethylprop-1-ene"],"a":1,"x":"The longest chain containing C=C has four carbons: CH₂=C(CH₃)–CH₂–CH₃. Number from the end that gives the double bond the lowest number (1), so the methyl group is on carbon 2."}
{"id":"ch-082","s":"chem","t":"3.3.1","lv":2,"q":"In (Z)-1-chloroprop-1-ene, CH₃CH=CHCl, which statement is correct?","o":["The Cl atom and the H atom on the other carbon are on the same side","The CH₃ group and the H atom on the other carbon are on the same side","The Cl atom and the CH₃ group are on the same side of the double bond","The two H atoms attached to the double bond are on opposite sides"],"a":2,"x":"Z means the higher priority groups are on the same side. On each carbon the higher priority group is Cl (over H) and CH₃ (over H). In the Z isomer Cl and CH₃ are together and the two H atoms are together."}
{"id":"ch-083","s":"chem","t":"3.3.1","lv":3,"q":"How many alkenes of molecular formula C₅H₁₀ are there, counting E/Z isomers separately and ignoring cyclic isomers?","o":["4","5","7","6"],"a":3,"x":"Pent-1-ene; (E)- and (Z)-pent-2-ene; 2-methylbut-1-ene; 3-methylbut-1-ene; 2-methylbut-2-ene. Only pent-2-ene has two different groups on both carbons of C=C, so only it gives E/Z isomers: 5 structures, 6 isomers."}
{"id": "ch-084", "s": "chem", "t": "8.rp", "lv": 1, "q": "A burette is read to ±0.05 cm³. A titre of 24.30 cm³ is found from an initial and a final reading. What is the percentage uncertainty in the titre?", "o": ["0.21%", "0.41%", "0.10%", "0.82%"], "a": 1, "x": "Two readings each carry ±0.05 cm³, so the titre is ±0.10 cm³. 0.10 ÷ 24.30 × 100 = 0.41%. The trap is counting only one reading, which gives 0.21%."}
{"id": "ch-085", "s": "chem", "t": "8.rp", "lv": 1, "q": "In a titration of an alkali with acid, which piece of apparatus can still be wet with distilled water before use without affecting the result?", "o": ["The burette, before it is filled with acid", "The 25.0 cm³ pipette, before it is filled with alkali", "The conical flask, before the alkali is added to it", "The burette, if the acid is more concentrated than the alkali"], "a": 2, "x": "The pipette fixes the moles of alkali in the flask, so extra water in the flask does not change them. Water left in the burette or pipette dilutes the solution it delivers and changes the result."}
{"id": "ch-086", "s": "chem", "t": "8.rp", "lv": 2, "q": "A student makes 250 cm³ of 0.0500 mol dm⁻³ sodium carbonate (Mr = 106.0) as a standard solution. What mass of Na₂CO₃ should be weighed out?", "o": ["1.33 g", "5.30 g", "2.65 g", "0.663 g"], "a": 0, "x": "Moles = 0.0500 × 0.250 = 0.0125 mol. Mass = 0.0125 × 106.0 = 1.325 g, which is 1.33 g. The trap is using 1 dm³ (5.30 g) instead of 250 cm³."}
{"id": "ch-087", "s": "chem", "t": "8.rp", "lv": 2, "q": "Burning 0.46 g of ethanol (Mr = 46.0) raises the temperature of 100 g of water by 20.0 K. c = 4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹. Calculate the enthalpy change of combustion of ethanol.", "o": ["−8.36 kJ mol⁻¹", "+836 kJ mol⁻¹", "−3.85 kJ mol⁻¹", "−836 kJ mol⁻¹"], "a": 3, "x": "q = 100 × 4.18 × 20.0 = 8360 J = 8.36 kJ. Moles ethanol = 0.0100, so ΔH = −836 kJ mol⁻¹ (exothermic). Traps: not dividing by moles, or using the mass of ethanol instead of water."}
{"id": "ch-088", "s": "chem", "t": "8.rp", "lv": 1, "q": "Marble chips react with excess hydrochloric acid in a flask on a balance, and the mass is recorded over time. Why is loose cotton wool placed in the neck of the flask?", "o": ["To stop CO₂ leaving so the mass stays constant", "To let CO₂ escape but stop acid spray being lost", "To absorb the CO₂ so the mass falls more quickly", "To cool the mixture and slow the reaction"], "a": 1, "x": "The mass loss is the CO₂ escaping, so the gas must be able to leave. Loose cotton wool stops droplets of acid spraying out, which would give a false extra mass loss."}
{"id": "ch-089", "s": "chem", "t": "8.rp", "lv": 2, "q": "A gas volume–time graph for a reaction is a curve through the origin. The tangent at t = 0 passes through (0 s, 0 cm³) and (30 s, 18 cm³). What is the initial rate of reaction?", "o": ["0.60 cm³ s⁻¹", "1.7 cm³ s⁻¹", "18 cm³ s⁻¹", "0.060 cm³ s⁻¹"], "a": 0, "x": "Rate = gradient of the tangent = 18 cm³ ÷ 30 s = 0.60 cm³ s⁻¹. Dividing time by volume (1.7) inverts the gradient. Using the volume alone (18) ignores the time."}
{"id": "ch-090", "s": "chem", "t": "8.rp", "lv": 1, "q": "In the test for sulfate ions, dilute hydrochloric acid is added before barium chloride solution. Why?", "o": ["To make barium sulfate more soluble so the precipitate is purer", "To supply the chloride ions needed to form the precipitate", "To remove carbonate ions, which would also give a white precipitate with Ba²⁺", "To speed up the precipitation of barium sulfate"], "a": 2, "x": "Carbonate ions would give white BaCO₃, a false positive. Acid reacts with carbonate and removes it. BaSO₄ is insoluble in acid, so only sulfate gives a precipitate that remains."}
{"id": "ch-091", "s": "chem", "t": "8.rp", "lv": 1, "q": "Acidified silver nitrate gives a cream precipitate. It is insoluble in dilute ammonia solution but dissolves in concentrated ammonia solution. Which halide ion is present?", "o": ["Cl⁻", "I⁻", "F⁻", "Br⁻"], "a": 3, "x": "Bromide gives a cream precipitate that dissolves only in concentrated ammonia. AgCl is white and dissolves in dilute ammonia. AgI is yellow and insoluble in both. AgF is soluble, so no precipitate forms."}
{"id": "ch-092", "s": "chem", "t": "8.rp", "lv": 2, "q": "Propan-1-ol is oxidised to propanoic acid by heating with acidified potassium dichromate(VI). Why is the mixture heated under reflux rather than in an open flask?", "o": ["Reflux raises the boiling point so the reaction is faster", "Vapours condense and return to the flask, so heating continues until complete", "Reflux removes the aldehyde as soon as it forms", "The condenser stops oxygen in the air reacting with the alcohol"], "a": 1, "x": "The condenser returns vaporised alcohol, aldehyde and water to the flask, so the mixture can be heated for a long time without loss. Removing the aldehyde as it forms is distillation, not reflux."}
{"id": "ch-093", "s": "chem", "t": "8.rp", "lv": 3, "q": "12.5 g of cyclohexanol (C₆H₁₂O, Mr = 100.0) is dehydrated to cyclohexene (C₆H₁₀, Mr = 82.0). After distillation, 5.74 g of cyclohexene is obtained. What is the percentage yield?", "o": ["45.9%", "37.7%", "56.0%", "44.0%"], "a": 2, "x": "Moles alcohol = 0.125, and the ratio is 1:1. Theoretical mass = 0.125 × 82.0 = 10.25 g. Yield = 5.74 ÷ 10.25 = 56.0%. Dividing by the starting mass (45.9%) ignores the change in Mr."}
{"id": "ch-094", "s": "chem", "t": "8.rp", "lv": 2, "q": "Four colourless liquids are each warmed with acidified potassium dichromate(VI). Which one stays orange?", "o": ["2-methylpropan-2-ol", "butan-2-ol", "butan-1-ol", "butanal"], "a": 0, "x": "Tertiary alcohols cannot be oxidised, so 2-methylpropan-2-ol stays orange. Primary alcohols, secondary alcohols and aldehydes are all oxidised, turning the dichromate from orange to green."}
{"id": "ch-095", "s": "chem", "t": "8.rp", "lv": 1, "q": "A student calculates n = 1.85 g ÷ 74.1 g mol⁻¹ = 0.0249662… mol. Both values are given to 3 significant figures. How should the answer be written?", "o": ["0.025 mol", "0.02497 mol", "0.0249662 mol", "0.0250 mol"], "a": 3, "x": "The answer should match the data, which is 3 significant figures: 0.0250 mol. The trailing zero is significant. 0.025 has only 2 s.f., and the other options keep too many digits from the calculator."}
{"id": "ch-096", "s": "chem", "t": "8.rp", "lv": 2, "q": "A balance reads to ±0.005 g. A weighing bottle is 5.62 g before and 4.37 g after solid is tipped out. What is the percentage uncertainty in the 1.25 g transferred?", "o": ["0.40%", "0.089%", "0.80%", "0.18%"], "a": 2, "x": "Mass by difference uses two readings, so the uncertainty is ±0.010 g. 0.010 ÷ 1.25 × 100 = 0.80%. Using one reading gives 0.40%, and dividing by the bottle mass is wrong."}
{"id": "ch-097", "s": "chem", "t": "8.rp", "lv": 3, "q": "25.0 cm³ portions of NaOH(aq) are titrated with 0.0500 mol dm⁻³ H₂SO₄. Titres: 23.15 (rough), 22.40, 22.35, 22.45 cm³. What is the concentration of the NaOH?", "o": ["0.0896 mol dm⁻³", "0.0448 mol dm⁻³", "0.0926 mol dm⁻³", "0.0558 mol dm⁻³"], "a": 0, "x": "Mean of concordant titres = 22.40 cm³. Moles H₂SO₄ = 1.12 × 10⁻³, so NaOH = 2.24 × 10⁻³ (2:1). Divide by 0.0250 dm³ to get 0.0896. Using the rough titre gives 0.0926, and ignoring the 2:1 ratio gives 0.0448."}
